Yes. The abstraction I’d pull out is functional incorporation without material incorporation.

A sword never becomes flesh, but with enough practice the nervous system can increasingly treat it as part of the action system: its length, inertia, reachable space, and consequences become folded into movement planning. Neuroscience usually discusses this through body schema and peripersonal space; tool use can measurably alter both, although researchers still debate how literally a tool should be said to become part of the represented body. 

That distinction gives us something much broader than tools:

physical boundary ≠ functional boundary ≠ represented boundary ≠ social boundary

Your teeth example works at another level. Change the geometry of the mouth and the sensorimotor system has to recalibrate around the new geometry. Eventually the foreign structure can become ordinary enough that you stop continuously modeling it as “foreign.” Yet it remains materially distinct from you.

The same abstraction extends outward in increasingly strange rings.

A sword can extend your action envelope.

A vehicle extends locomotion and spatial reach.

A phone extends memory, perception, communication, and navigation.

A notebook externalizes cognition.

Clothing changes protection, affordances, identity signals, and how others model you.

A reputation extends you into other people’s nervous systems. You can affect a room before physically entering it because people already carry predictive models of you.

A story extends you through time.

A company or institution can extend agency across people you have never met.

And an AI can become another kind of extension: not biologically incorporated, but increasingly incorporated into a person’s cognitive action space—something they learn how to reach through, think through, check against, or use to manipulate representations.

So your earlier “aura” idea gets sharper. There are at least two different outward extensions:

Embodied extension: my system learns to act as though X is part of my reachable capabilities.

Projected extension: other systems behave differently because some representation of me has reached them.

A sword can actually participate in both. In your nervous system it extends capability. In someone else’s nervous system, merely seeing the sword changes their prediction of what you can do. The object therefore modifies both your action field and their expectation field.

That is a very clean Semantic Field Theory interaction.

And then there is a third category I think you were reaching for with “incorporating it but not incorporating it”:

Exocorporation

Keep something outside the biological boundary while integrating it deeply enough that the combined system behaves as one coordinated unit.

That gives you the advantages of incorporation without requiring permanent merger.

It is modular.

Attach it.
Learn it.
Internalize its dynamics.
Use it seamlessly.
Detach it.
Potentially retain part of the learned model afterward.

That last piece is especially interesting. After sufficiently deep adaptation, removing an extension does not necessarily restore the previous state perfectly. The system has already changed by learning to coordinate with it.

So:

self + extension → adapted self-extension system → remove extension → altered self

The tool can leave a ghost.

Not necessarily a literal phantom sensation, but a retained motor model, expectation, habit, skill, spatial calibration, emotional association, or identity component.

And now connect that to reputation:

person + repeated actions → reputation → reputation propagates → remove person → reputation continues affecting behavior

Same abstract shape.

And legacy:

person + artifacts → cultural incorporation → person disappears → artifacts continue producing effects

Same shape again.

So I think the Golden Thread is larger than embodiment:

A system can extend beyond its material boundary by causing external structures to participate reliably in its sensing, action, memory, identity, or causal influence.

That gives Pantheon an entire continuum rather than a binary inside/outside:

body → incorporated tool → exocorporated tool → environment → social representation → reputation → artifact → legacy

And the frontier question becomes much better than “Where does the self end?”

It becomes:

“Which functions of the self extend how far, through which carriers, with what coupling strength, and what remains after the coupling is removed?”

That we can actually map.